{
  "rules": [
    "Systematic Underestimation of Bullish Spikes: The model consistently fails to capture sudden upward price breaks (e.g., the jump from ~80 to ~87-88), forecasting only a slow, linear drift. Correction: When sentiment history shows a cluster of high values (>=0.67) followed by a price breakout, apply a non-linear upward adjustment to the forecast horizon 1-5 days.",
    "Lag in Mean Reversion: The model reacts too slowly to price drops following a spike, maintaining elevated forecasts while true prices fall sharply (e.g., dropping from 91 to 83). Correction: Accelerate the downward slope in the forecast immediately after a peak is confirmed, reducing the forecast by 3-5% per day until the new lower mean is reached.",
    "Sentiment-Price Decoupling: High sentiment scores (1.00) often precede immediate price surges that the model misses entirely, treating them as noise rather than trend signals. Correction: Weight recent sentiment history more heavily than the last 18 days of price data when sentiment is consistently positive; ignore the model's default mean-reversion logic during these periods.",
    "Horizon-Specific Bias: The model exhibits a 'flat-line' bias, predicting a near-constant price regardless of the 30-day horizon. Correction: Introduce a volatility multiplier that increases the forecast range (both up and down) as the horizon extends, specifically widening the band after any price movement exceeding 2%.",
    "Overshoot Correction: After the model eventually catches up to a new price level, it tends to overshoot the subsequent correction phase. Correction: Apply a dampening factor to the forecast slope once the predicted price exceeds the recent 10-day high by more than 1.5 standard deviations."
  ]
}
